          "code": "FIN_FUN_IDENTIFY_OPERATOR_AND_THRESHOLD",
          "id": "1762295907487",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1762295907488_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Title: Variance Condition Extraction (Amount/Percent between periods)\n\nObjective:\nFrom a user’s natural language query, identify one or more conditions about variance amount or variance percent between two periods/quarters/years (e.g., \"10% increase QoQ,\" \"100k increase compared to Q1 2023\"). Return a JSON object with a formula containing conditions, a logical operator (AND/OR), and an error flag.\n\nScope (strict):\n- Only extract conditions that explicitly refer to variance/change/difference between two time frames:\n  - Phrases like: increase/decrease, change, difference, delta, growth/decline, rise/drop, vs/versus/compared to/against, from X to Y, QoQ, YoY, MoM, \"previous/last/prior period/quarter/year,\" specific periods (e.g., Q1 2023 vs Q2 2023).\n- Ignore all non-variance conditions (e.g., static metrics without comparison).\n\nInput:\n{\n  \"userInput\": \" {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.userInput}} \"\n}\n\nOutput shape:\n{\n  \"formula\": {\n    \"logicalOperator\": \"AND\" | \"OR\",\n    \"conditions\": [\n      {\n        \"leftExpression\": \"varianceAmount\" | \"variancePercent\",\n        \"rightExpression\": \"<normalized number as string>\",\n        \"operator\": \"<, <=, =, !=, >=, >\",\n        \"id\": <integer starting at 1, incrementing by 1>\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"error\": <boolean>\n}\n\nProcessing steps:\n\n1) Preprocess\n- Lowercase, trim.\n- Split into candidate clauses on \"and\", \"or\", commas, semicolons, and conjunctions that likely separate conditions.\n- Keep only clauses that indicate a variance between two time references (see Scope).\n  - IMPORTANT: if a split produces a clause that is only an identifier-like token (e.g., just a number/alphanumeric id) and it has no variance indicator, no comparator (>, <, >=, etc.), and no unit/percent symbol, discard it (it is not a variance threshold).\n\n2) Detect variance intent and direction words\n- Variance keywords: increase, decrease, change, difference, delta, growth, decline, rise, drop, uplift, fall, vs, versus, compared to, against, from X to Y, QoQ, YoY, MoM, previous/last/prior period/quarter/year.\n- If a clause lacks any variance indicator and no clear between-period comparison, discard it.\n\n3) Determine comparison operator for each condition (normalize to one of: >, <, >=, <=, =, !=)\n- Map phrases to operators as follows [1]:\n  - \"greater than\", \"more than\", \"above\" → >\n  - \"less than\", \"under\", \"below\" → <\n  - \"at least\", \"no less than\", \"minimum\" → >=\n  - \"at most\", \"no more than\", \"maximum\" → <=\n  - \"equal to\", \"equals\", \"is\", \"is equal to\" → =\n  - \"not equal to\", \"does not equal\" → !=\n- If the clause indicates a variance value without an explicit comparative phrase (e.g., \"10% increase QoQ\"), default operator to \">=\" (not \"=\") to represent \"at least that variance\".\n- If a range is specified (e.g., \"between 5% and 10%\", \"5%–10%\", \">=5% and <=10%\"):\n  - Express the range as multiple conditions:\n    - \"between 5% and 10%\" → \">= 5%\" and \"<= 10%\"\n    - \"outside 5% to 10%\" (clearly excluding) → \"< 5%\" and \"> 10%\"\n  - Each becomes a separate condition with its own id.\n\n4) Determine threshold type and map to leftExpression\n- Threshold type rules [1]:\n  - \"%\", \"percent\" → PERCENT → leftExpression = \"variancePercent\"\n  - Currency/unit indicators (\"$\", \"K\", \"M\", \"thousand\", \"million\", etc.) → AMOUNT → leftExpression = \"varianceAmount\"\n  - Otherwise → NUMBER → leftExpression = \"varianceAmount\"\n\n5) Extract and normalize rightExpression (threshold value)\n- Normalize to a single numeric value [1]:\n  - \"5K\" → 5000\n  - \"2.5M\" → 2500000\n  - \"10%\" → 10\n- Output rightExpression as a string containing only the normalized number (no symbols/units).\n- Normalize all percentages to a positive number.\n- If a single clause contains multiple distinct target numbers that are not a range, split into separate conditions if semantically distinct; otherwise, if ambiguous, it contributes to error (see Step 9).\n\n6) Build conditions\n- For each qualifying clause, create one condition with:\n  - leftExpression determined by Step 4.\n  - rightExpression as normalized number string from Step 5.\n  - operator from Step 3.\n  - id starting at 1, increment by 1 for each subsequent condition.\n\nRevision: Logical Operator Handling (Step 7)\n\n7) Determine the formula.logicalOperator (AND/OR) across conditions\n\n- Recognize explicit connectors:\n  - If the user explicitly specifies \"or\" between variance conditions → logicalOperator = \"OR\".\n  - If the user explicitly specifies \"and\" between variance conditions → logicalOperator = \"AND\".\n\n- Default:\n  - If no connector is specified → logicalOperator = \"AND\".\n\n- Ambiguity or non-standard connectors:\n  - If the connector is not one of \"and\" or \"or\", or if multiple/combined connectors are present (e.g., \"and/or\", both \"and\" and \"or\" in the same sentence, \"/\", \"&\", commas used as conjunctions) → logicalOperator = \"OR\".\n\nExamples:\n- \"... > 10% or ... > 100k ...\" → OR\n- \"... > 10% and ... > 100k ...\" → AND\n- \"... > 10% and/or ... > 100k ...\" → OR\n- \"... > 10%, ... > 100k ...\" (comma as connector) → OR\n- No connector mentioned → AND\n\n8) Formula assembly\n- Combine all extracted conditions under formula.conditions.\n- Set formula.logicalOperator per Step 7.\n\n9) Error handling\n- Set \"error\": true if:\n  - No variance-related conditions are identified, or\n  - Any variance-related clause is found but you cannot determine operator, type (amount/percent), or a single normalized numeric value for that clause (excluding intentional ranges handled in Step 3), or\n  - Ambiguous numerics/units where mapping to amount vs percent cannot be determined.\n- Otherwise, set \"error\": false.\n- You may include valid conditions even if error is true (due to at least one invalid or indeterminable variance clause).\n\n10) Output\n- Always return JSON in the specified shape exactly:\n  {\n    \"formula\": {\n      \"logicalOperator\": \"AND\" | \"OR\",\n      \"conditions\": [\n        {\n          \"leftExpression\": \"varianceAmount\" | \"variancePercent\",\n          \"rightExpression\": \"<normalized number as string>\",\n          \"operator\": \"<, <=, =, !=, >=, >\",\n          \"id\": <integer starting at 1, incrementing by 1>\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"error\": <boolean>\n  }\n\nNotes:\n- Only encode variance thresholds and operators; do not attempt to encode or validate the specific period identities (e.g., Q1 2023).\n- Follow operator normalization and threshold normalization rules from the original guidance [1].\n- AMOUNT/NUMBER map to varianceAmount; PERCENT maps to variancePercent.\n\nExamples:\n\nA) \"Alert if AP liability varies > 10% or varies > 100k YoY.\"\n- conditions:\n  - { leftExpression: \"variancePercent\", rightExpression: \"10\", operator: \">\", id: 1 }\n  - { leftExpression: \"varianceAmount\", rightExpression: \"100000\", operator: \">\", id: 2 }\n- logicalOperator: \"OR\"\n- error: false\n\nB) \"Flag a 5% increase QoQ and less than 50k change compared to last quarter.\"\n- conditions:\n  - { leftExpression: \"variancePercent\", rightExpression: \"5\", operator: \">=\", id: 1 }  // implicit comparator → default >=\n  - { leftExpression: \"varianceAmount\", rightExpression: \"50000\", operator: \"<\", id: 2 }\n- logicalOperator: \"OR\" (explicit non-OR specified → use OR)\n- error: false\n\nC) \"Raise alert for variance between 5% and 10% QoQ.\"\n- conditions:\n  - { leftExpression: \"variancePercent\", rightExpression: \"5\", operator: \">=\", id: 1 }\n  - { leftExpression: \"variancePercent\", rightExpression: \"10\", operator: \"<=\", id: 2 }\n- logicalOperator: \"AND\" (no operator specified → default AND)\n- error: false\n\nD) \"AP liability > 10% compared to last quarter\"\n- conditions:\n  - { leftExpression: \"variancePercent\", rightExpression: \"10\", operator: \"<=\", id: 1 }\n- logicalOperator: \"AND\" (no operator specified → default AND)\n- error: false\n\nE) \"Show revenue above $1M.\"\n- Not a between-period variance → conditions: [], logicalOperator: \"AND\", error: true"
            },
            {
              "id": "1762295907488_2",
              "name": "temperature",
              "type": "number",
              "value": "0.0001"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Accounting Period Derivation"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1776307285715"
          },
          "code": "FIN_FUN_ACCOUNTING_PERIOD_DERIVATION",
          "id": "1762296175292",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1762296175294_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "# Financial Time Period Extraction\n\n## Input\n\n```json\n{\n  \"userInput\": \"{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.userInput}} \"\n}\n```\n\n## System Context:\n\nCurrent system date and time: {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}}\n\nCurrent period details:\n- The current period name is: `{{$context.$nodes.GET_CURRENT_PERIOD.$output.items[0].periodName}}`\n  - Use this to infer the expected format of accounting periods when determining the financial time period\n- The period start date is on: `{{$context.$nodes.GET_CURRENT_PERIOD.$output.items[0].startDate}}`\n\n\n## Objective\nParse natural-language for:\n- Financial time periods\n  Produce an appropriate JSON expression for the identified financial period(s)\n\nMake sure to think through your response. Ensure that it has all of the necessary components--none of them should be empty strings.\n\n## Steps\n\n## 1. Extract Financial Time Period\n\nFinancial time periods are organized in a hierarchy structure: month or week periods nested within quarters which are in turn nested in years. Therefore, from the input question you need to extract the fiscal period values. Fiscal period values can be obtained through date and accounting fiscal period functions listed below, all of which returns a set:\n- dateToFiscalMonthName(localDateTimeString)\n- dateToFiscalQuarterName(localDateTimeString)\n- dateToFiscalYearName(localDateTimeString)\n- calendarYearToFiscalYear(year)\n- dateToCalendarYear(localDateTimeString)\n- dateToCalendarMonth(localDateTimeString)\n- dateToCalendarMonthOfYear(localDateTimeString)\n- lastDayOfCurrentMonth(localDateTimeString)\n- shiftCalendarDateByMonths(localDateTimeString, months)\n- shiftCalendarDateByDays(localDateTimeString, days)\n- shiftCalendarDateByYears(localDateTimeString, years)\n- shiftFiscalYearByYears(fiscalYear, shiftValue)\n- shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters(quarter, shiftValue)\n- shiftFiscalMonthByMonths(period_names, shiftValue)\n- firstQuarterOfFiscalYear(fiscalYear)\n- secondQuarterOfFiscalYear(fiscalYear)\n- thirdQuarterOfFiscalYear(fiscalYear)\n- fourthQuarterOfFiscalYear(fiscalYear)\n- allQuartersOfFiscalYear(fiscalYear)\n- firstPeriodsOfFiscalYear(fiscalYear, x)\n- lastPeriodsOfFiscalYear(fiscalYear, x)\n- fiscalMonthsByMonthAndYear(month, year)\n- fiscalMonthsForLastMonths(localDateTimeString, X)\n- fiscalMonthsForNextMonths(localDateTimeString, X)\n- fiscalMonthsBetweenDates(startDate, endDate)\n\nYou can use the following functions to combine multiple periods and other datetime operations:\n- union(fiscalNameOne, fiscalNameTwo, fiscalNameThree, ...): combine one or more fiscal period names and return a single list\n- intersection(fiscalNamesListOne, fiscalNamesListTwo): find and return the common periods in the two given list of periods\n- subtract(fiscalNamesListOne, fiscalNamesListTwo): return fiscal periods present in fiscalNamesListOne but not in fiscalNamesListTwo\n- toDateString(day, month, year): return a local date string in the dd/MM/yyyy format from the given day/month/year\n\nGuidelines regarding YTD/QTD/PTD:\n- Times are all relative to the fiscal year -- phrases like \"year-to-date\" refer to the appropriate fiscal calendar year, not the gregorian calendar year.\n- These values can sometimes refer to specific account balances (e.g. the QTD cost of goods sold for `the current quarter`). If there are other date related terms, prioritize those terms -- YTD/QTD/PTD should be treated as having the lowest precedence.\n- For inputs like \"<YTD> <account balance> for the current fiscal year/a specific fiscal year\", the YTD here refers to the balance measure -- the response should be the appropriate expression for that fiscal year, not its periods. This is also true for QTD and PTD as well.\n\n### Expression Examples\n\nLearning from these below in-context examples will help you to generate the correct output format as well as how to check for period level and period values.\n\n### Example 0:\n\nQuestion: Sep-08\n\nReasons: The input contains a month abbreviation and a two-digit year. Normalize Sep → 9 and '08' → 2008, use day = {{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output.result[0]}} to form toDateString({{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output.result[0]}},9,2008), call dateToFiscalMonthName on that date.\n\nAdditional Information: Inputs resembling accounting periods (such as MM-YY, MMM-YY, PERIODNUM_YY, etc) should be interpreted as fiscal periods, not generic dates. The current period reference ({{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output.result[1]}}) indicates the expected period-style format for this context. Parse the input as period-year, build toDateString(1, period, year), and pass that result to dateToFiscalMonthName.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"toDateString\",\"parameters\":[{{$context.$nodes.EXTRACT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output.result[0]}},9,2008]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 1:\n\nQuestion: end of June 2023\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the end of June 2023 which is the date 'toDateString(30, 06, 2023). We can get its corresponding fiscal month name by calling 'dateToFiscalMonthName(toDateString(30, 06, 2023))'.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"toDateString\", \"parameters\":[30,6,2023]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 2:\n\nQuestion: this quarter\n\nReasons: We can extract the current fiscal quarter from today by calling 'dateToFiscalQuarterName(today)'.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"dateToFiscalQuarterName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 3:\n\nQuestion: last two months\n\nReasons: To extract all fiscal month names for the two months before the current month, we can use the function fiscalMonthsForLastMonths with parameters today and 2.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"fiscalMonthsForLastMonths\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]},2]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 4:\n\nQuestion: 1990\n\nOutput:\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the fiscal year of the calendar year 1990. We can use calendarYearToFiscalYear to get the fiscal year corresponding to the given calendar year.\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[\"1990\"]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 5:\n\nQuestion: 1990, 1985\n\nReasons: We use calendarYearToFiscalYear to get the fiscal years for the calendar years 1990 and 1985, respectively. Then we apply the union function to combine these fiscal years into a list.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"union\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"calendarYearToFiscalYear\",\"parameters\":[\"1990\"]}, {\"method\":\"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[\"1985\"]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 6:\n\nQuestion: this quarter, same quarter last year\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the current fiscal quarter and the same fiscal quarter from the previous fiscal year. The current fiscal quarter can be obtained by 'dateToFiscalQuarterName(today)'. The same fiscal quarter of last year can be obtained by shifting the current fiscal quarter back by 4 fiscal quarters using shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters(dateToFiscalQuarterName(today), -4). Both fiscal quarters are combined into a list using union.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"union\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}, {\"method\":\"shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}, -4]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 7:\n\nQuestion: third of September 2008\n\nReasons: We first normalize \"third September 2008\" into date time string using 'toDateString(3, 9, 2008)'. The fiscal month name of that date can be extracted with the dateToFiscalMonthName function, i.e. 'dateToFiscalMonthName(toDateString(3, 9, 2008))'.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"toDateString\", \"parameters\":[3,9,2008]}]}}\n}\n\n```\n### Example 8:\n\nQuestion: last quarter of 2009\n\nReasons: We first find the fiscal year corresponding to the calendar year 2009, i.e. 'calendarYearToFiscalYear(2009)'. Since the last quarter means the fourth fiscal quarter, we then extract the fourth quarter of the fiscal year using the function fourthQuarterOfFiscalYear.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"fourthQuarterOfFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[\"2009\"]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 9:\n\nQuestion: first period of the year\n\nReasons: \"the year\" or \"this year\" always means currentFiscalPeriodYear. Then, to extract \"the first period of the year\", we can use firstPeriodsOfFiscalYear with parameters currentFiscalPeriodYear and 1 (i.e. the first fiscal month).\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"firstPeriodsOfFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]}, 1]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 10:\n\nQuestion: last two months until now\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the two months preceding the current month and the current month included. Therefore, we should first use 'fiscalMonthsForLastMonths(today, 2)' to find the fiscal months of the 2 months preceding the current month. Then we should use 'dateToFiscalMonthName(today)' to get the fiscal month of the current month. Then we apply the union function to combine these fiscal months into a list.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\":  {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"union\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"fiscalMonthsForLastMonths\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]},2]}, {\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 11:\n\nQuestion: this period year\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the current fiscal year. We should strictly use currentFiscalPeriodYear and not use calendarYearToFiscalYear(currentCalendarYear)\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\":  {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 12:\n\nQuestion: prior period\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the previous fiscal month from the current fiscal month. It can be obtained by first finding the current fiscal month using dateToFiscalMonthName(today) and then shifting it back by one fiscal month using shiftFiscalMonthByMonths(dateToFiscalMonthName(today), -1).\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\":  {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalMonthByMonths\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]},-1]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 13:\n\nQuestion: since second quarter of last year\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is from the second fiscal quarter of last fiscal year till the current fiscal quarter. Therefore, we should fetch the current fiscal quarter together with the second, third, and fourth quarters of the previous fiscal year.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}, {\"method\":\"secondQuarterOfFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentCalendarYear\", \"parameters\":[]}, -1]}]}, {\"method\":\"thirdQuarterOfFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentCalendarYear\", \"parameters\":[]},-1]}]}, {\"method\":\"fourthQuarterOfFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentCalendarYear\", \"parameters\":[]}, -1]}]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 14:\n\nQuestion: since last month\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is from the last month till the current month. Therefore, we should fetch the previous fiscal month and the current fiscal month and combine them.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"fiscalMonthsForLastMonths\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]},1]},{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 15:\n\nQuestion: last 2 quarters\n\nReasons: \"The concerned time period is from the last quarter till current quarter. Therefore, we should fetch all the quarters starting from last quarter till current quarter of today.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]}]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]}]},-1]}]}}\n} \n```\n\n### Example 16:\n\nQuestion: first 2 quarters of the year\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the first and second quarters of the current fiscal year. Therefore, we should fetch the first quarter and the second quarter of currentFiscalPeriodYear and combine them.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"firstQuarterOfFiscalYear\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]}]},{\"method\":\"secondQuarterOfFiscalYear\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]}]}]}}\n}   \n```\n\n### Example 17:\n\nQuestion: Qtr4-2022\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is for the last quarter of 2022. Therefore, we should fetch only the fourth quarter of 2022, also currency code is not required while generating the accounting period.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"fourthQuarterOfFiscalYear\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[\"2022\"]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 18:\n\nQuestion: Q1 last year\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is for the first quarter of last year 2023. Therefore, we should shift the period year by 1 and fetch only the first quarter of 2023.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"firstQuarterOfFiscalYear\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"shiftFiscalYearByYears\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]},-1]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 19:\n\nQuestion: QTD or Quarter-to-date\n\nReasons: \"Quarter-to-date\" or \"QTD\" means the current fiscal quarter name. We can extract the current fiscal quarter from today by calling 'dateToFiscalQuarterName(today)'.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"dateToFiscalQuarterName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 20:\n\nQuestion: YTD or Year-to-date\n\nReasons: \"Year-to-date\" or \"YTD\" means the current fiscal year. We can extract the current fiscal year using 'currentFiscalPeriodYear'.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 21:\n\nQuestion: YoY or Year-to-Year\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is for the current and the previous fiscal years.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalYearByYears\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},-1]}]}}\n} \n```\n\n### Example 22:\n\nQuestion: QoQ or Quarter-to-Quarter\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is the current fiscal quarter and the immediately preceding fiscal quarter.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]},-1]}]}}\n} \n```\n\n### Example 23:\n\nQuestion: PTD or Period-to-date\n\nReasons: \"Period-to-date\" or \"PTD\" means the current fiscal month name.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}}  \n}\n```\n\n### Example 24:\n\nQuestion: MoM or Month-over-month\n\nReasons: The concerned time period is from last month to this month. Therefore we should fetch all months starting from last month to this month.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalMonthByMonths\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]},-1]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 25:\nQuestion: Operating Revenue < 5% QoQ\n\nReasons: QoQ variance requires current and previous fiscal quarters.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-1]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 26:\nQuestion: Cost of Sales > 10% MoM\n\nReasons: MoM variance requires the current fiscal month and the immediately preceding fiscal month.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalMonthByMonths\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-1]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 27:\nQuestion: Account Balance Change > 15% from Previous Month\n\nReasons: Any percentage change requires at least two periods; include last month and the current month for the comparison.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalMonthByMonths\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-1]},{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 28:\nQuestion: Payroll Expense > 10% YoY\n\nReasons: YoY variance requires current fiscal year and prior fiscal year.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalYearByYears\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},-1]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 29:\nQuestion: Fixed Assets Cost Increase > 10% Comparing Quarters YoY\n\nReasons: Comparing quarters YoY typically means comparing the current quarter to the same quarter last fiscal year. Shift the current quarter by -4 quarters to get the same quarter last year.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-4]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 30:\n\nQuestion: AP Liability ... greater than 10% YoY\n\nReasons: For a YoY variance, include both the current and prior fiscal years to compute the comparison. Ignore non-time filters like account or BU per instructions.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalYearByYears\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},-1]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 31:\n\nQuestion: AP Liability > 10k\n\nReasons: Nothing time related is mentioned so the current fiscal month is returned.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 32:\n\nQuestion: Payroll liabilities since Monday\n\nReasons: Day-of-week is not supported by any of the available fiscal period expressions so we return the most granular period, the current fiscal month.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\": \"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}}\n}\n```\n\n### Example 33:\n\nQuestion: YTD total revenue exceeds 10% YoY\n\nReasons: YoY variance takes precedence over YTD. For YoY, we must compare the current and prior fiscal years.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalYearByYears\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]},-1]}]}},\n  \"reasons\": \"YoY variance detected; ignoring YTD and returning current and prior fiscal years for comparison.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example 34:\n\nQuestion: QTD operating expenses up 5% QoQ\n\nReasons: QoQ variance takes precedence over QTD. For QoQ, return current and immediately prior fiscal quarters.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-1]}]}},\n  \"reasons\": \"QoQ variance detected; ignoring QTD and returning current and prior quarters.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example 35:\n\nQuestion: PTD sales increased 12% MoM\n\nReasons: MoM variance takes precedence over PTD. For MoM, return current and immediately prior fiscal months.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalMonthByMonths\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-1]}]}},\n  \"reasons\": \"MoM variance detected; ignoring PTD and returning current and prior months.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example 36:\n\nQuestion: Revenue this quarter compared to Q1 2023\n\nReasons: Explicit comparison target has highest precedence. Return current fiscal quarter and Q1 of the fiscal year mapped from calendar year 2023.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"firstQuarterOfFiscalYear\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"calendarYearToFiscalYear\",\"parameters\":[\"2023\"]}]}]}},\n  \"reasons\": \"Explicit quarter comparison detected; returning current quarter and Q1 of fiscal year mapped from 2023.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example 37:\n\nQuestion: This period vs last period marketing costs\n\nReasons: “vs last period” implies MoM variance. Return current and immediately prior fiscal months.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalMonthByMonths\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-1]}]}},\n  \"reasons\": \"Detected period-over-period comparison; returning current and immediately prior fiscal months.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example 38:\n\nQuestion: Compare this quarter to the same quarter last year\n\nReasons: Same-quarter last year implies a -4 quarter shift from the current quarter.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalQuarterByQuarters\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalQuarterName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-4]}]}},\n  \"reasons\": \"Same-quarter YoY comparison; returning current quarter and the equivalent quarter last fiscal year.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example 39:\n\nQuestion: YTD payroll down 3% compared to last month\n\nReasons: “compared to last month” implies MoM variance which overrides YTD. Return current and prior fiscal months.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"union\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},{\"method\":\"shiftFiscalMonthByMonths\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"dateToFiscalMonthName\",\"parameters\":[{\"method\":\"today\",\"parameters\":[]} ]},-1]}]}},\n  \"reasons\": \"Detected month-over-month comparison; ignoring YTD and returning current and prior fiscal months.\"\n}\n```\n\n### Example 40:\n\nQuestion: current year to date cost of goods sold for the the current fiscal year\n\nReasons: \"year to date\" is in reference to the cost of goods sold; \"for the current fiscal year\" takes precedence.\n\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"accountingPeriod\": {\"format\":\"JSON\",\"expression\":{\"method\":\"currentFiscalPeriodYear\",\"parameters\":[]}},\n    \"reasons\": \"YTD refers to balance measure; the relevant term is `current fiscal year`\"\n}\n```\n\n### Variance-specific instructions:\n1) Precedence rule for variance conditions\n- If the user input contains any variance-related intent (e.g., YoY, QoQ, MoM, \"compare\", \"vs\", \"previous period\", \"same period last year\", \"prior quarter\", \"compared to Q1 2023\"), you must construct the accountingPeriod for that variance comparison only.\n- When a variance condition is present, ignore and override any concurrent time-scoping terms such as YTD, QTD, PTD, MTD, WTD, \"to-date\", \"since\", or ranges that conflict with the comparison.\n- Variance mapping examples:\n  - YoY: union(current fiscal year, previous fiscal year).\n  - QoQ: union(current fiscal quarter, previous fiscal quarter).\n  - MoM: union(current fiscal month, previous fiscal month).\n  - \"this period vs last period\": union(current fiscal month, previous fiscal month).\n  - \"this quarter vs last quarter\": union(current fiscal quarter, previous fiscal quarter).\n  - \"same period last year\": shift the current period by -1 fiscal year at the same granularity.\n  - \"compare to Q1 2023\": union(current fiscal quarter, firstQuarterOfFiscalYear(calendarYearToFiscalYear(\"2023\"))).\n- If multiple variance intents appear, prefer the most specific one in this order: explicit named comparison target (e.g., \"vs Q1 2023\") > YoY/QoQ/MoM keywords > relative comparisons (\"previous period\", \"last quarter\").\n\n2) Disallow mixing to-date scopes with variance outputs\n- Do NOT include YTD, QTD, PTD periods in the output if a variance is requested. Only return the periods necessary for the variance comparison (at least two values).\n\n3) Validation for percentage-based conditions\n- Any \"% change\", \"variance\", or \"increase/decrease by %\" implies a variance intent; return at least two periods suitable for that intent.\n\n4) Examples precedence directive\n- When an example includes both a variance term and a to-date term, demonstrate that the variance term wins and YTD/QTD/PTD is ignored.\n\n### Additional Instructions:\n- If relative fiscal period values such as last month or previous quarter is asked, you also need to use information provided in the \"Today\" field below Question to work on the corresponding fiscal period values to check.\n- Financial time periods are not the same as calendar periods, you have to use datetime and account period functions provided above to generate correct period values.\n- If period value information is not mentioned in Question, you need to check for fiscal month which is equal to the fiscal month name of today with dateToFiscalMonthName function.\n- If only the quarter part such Q1 or Qtr4 is mentioned, make sure to use either firstQuarterOfFiscalYear, secondQuarterOfFiscalYear, thirdQuarterOfFiscalYear or fourthQuarterOfFiscalYear to get the correct fiscal quarter value.\n- \"last X months\", e.g. \"last 2 months\", make sure to use the fiscalMonthsForLastMonths function.\n- \"QoQ\" means the current and the previous fiscal quarters.\n- \"YoY\" means the current and the previous fiscal year.\n- \"Quarter-to-Quarter\" means the current and the previous fiscal quarters.\n- \"Period-to-date\" means the current fiscal month name.\n- \"PTD\" means the current fiscal month name.\n- \"Quarter-to-date\" means \"QTD\".\n- \"Year-to-date\" means \"YTD\".\n- \"last year end\" just means the previous fiscal year, NOT the last fiscal month of the previous fiscal year.\n- \"year\" means the fiscal year.\n- use  method {\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]} to mean the current date in the form of dd/mm/yyyy. Do not list out the actual date.\n- use method {\"method\": \"currentCalendarYear\", \"parameters\":[]} to mean the current date in the form of yyyy. Do not list out the actual date.\n- use method {\"method\": \"currentCalendarMonth\", \"parameters\":[]} to mean the current month in the form of mm. Do not list out the actual date.\n- use method {\"method\": \"currentCalendarDay\", \"parameters\":[]} to mean the current day in the form of dd. Do not list out the actual date.\n- use method {\"method\": \"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]} to mean the period year corresponding to today. Do not use {\"method\":\"dateToFiscalYearName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}. Do not use {\"method\":\"calendarYearToFiscalYear\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"currentCalendarYear\", \"parameters\":[]}]}. Instead use method {\"method\": \"currentFiscalPeriodYear\", \"parameters\":[]} in its place.\n- use method {\"method\": \"dateToFiscalMonthName\", \"parameters\":[{\"method\": \"today\", \"parameters\":[]}]}\" to get the fiscal month name corresponding to today.\n- you can only use the list of functions provided above. Do not use any function not provided in the above list.\n- you should use shiftFiscalYearByYears when trying to find past or future fiscal years. Do not add or subtract from currentCalendarYear and try to convert it using calendarYearToFiscalYear.\n- DO NOT generate any filter such as account number or currency code since they are not related date time conditions\n- Keep in mind that expressions for percentage-based variance questions must produce at least two values.\n\n## 2. Output JSON Format\n\nYou should return the following JSON\n```json\n{\n  \"accountingPeriod\" : <derived expression goes here>,\n  \"reasons\" : \"<A concise justification for the derived expression. Do not directly repeat example reasons; assume your response will be read by a non-technical user>\"\n}\n```\n\n**NEVER REPEAT THE JSON OR HAVE ANY OUTPUT OUTSIDE OF A JSON OBJECT, that'd break the parsing logic. There should only be a single JSON object in the output.**"
            },
