        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "GL Timespan & Frequency Extraction"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1772839528924"
          },
          "code": "FIN_FUN_GL_TIMESPAN_FREQUENCY_EXTRACTION",
          "id": "1772839437236",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1772839437237_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "# Timespan & Frequency Extraction\n\n## Input\n\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\" : \"{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timespan}}\",\n    \"frequency\" : \"{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.frequency}}\",\n    \"timezone\" : \"{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}}\"\n}\n```\n\n## System Context:\nCurrent system date and time:  {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}}\nCurrent period details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_CURRENT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output}}\nNext period details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_NEXT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output}}\nFirst period of next quarter details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_FIRST_PERIOD_NEXT_QUARTER_DETAILS.$output}}\nFirst period of next year details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_FIRST_PERIOD_NEXT_YEAR_DETAILS.$output}}\n\n## Objective\nParse natural-language for:\n- Schedule execution time\n- Timezone\n- Derived timespan (this MUST be included)\n- Frequency code awareness\n\nThis prompt runs in parallel with another that determines the cron schedule (including day-of-week/day-of-month/quarter/year). Do NOT duplicate scheduling semantics here. Treat schedule-related phrases (e.g., \"on the 15th\", \"at noon\", \"every Tuesday\") as inputs for executionTime or for the cron schedule to handle, not as a reason to emit a periodic timespan.\n\nYou will support:\n- Periodic timespans: recurring reporting windows relative to fiscal boundaries (GL_PERIOD_END_DATE, GL_QUARTER_END_DATE, GL_YEAR_END_DATE).\n- Static timespans: fixed start and end dates.\n- Unbounded timespans: no explicit reporting window (typical when only a schedule is specified).\n\n## 1. Identify Execution Time\n- Resolve the source timezone for interpretation:\n  - If the user explicitly specifies a timezone in the \"timespan\" text (e.g., PST, CET, UTC±hh:mm, IANA like America/Los_Angeles), use that.\n  - Otherwise, use {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}}.\n  - If neither is available or parsing fails, default to UTC.\n- Parse a local wall-clock time from the \"timespan\" text, recognizing numeric and natural-language forms:\n  - Numeric: \"8am\", \"08:00\", \"20:15\", \"5:30 pm\", \"noon\" => 12:00, \"midnight\" => 00:00.\n  - Minute-of-hour phrases (assume hour=00 when only a minute is given):\n    - \"the 5th minute of every hour\" => 00:05\n    - \"a quarter past the hour\" => 00:15\n    - \"half past the hour\" => 00:30\n    - \"a quarter to the hour\" => 00:45\n    - \"x minutes past the hour\" => 00:MM\n    - \"x minutes to the hour\" => 00:(60−x), zero-padded\n    - \"top of the hour\" => 00:00\n- If no time is found, default the local time to 00:00.\n- Convert the interpreted local time to UTC (cron executes in UTC):\n  - Apply the resolved timezone (including any DST rules) to the parsed local time.\n  - Convert the result to UTC.\n- Set executionTime to the resulting UTC time in zero-padded HH:MM format (no timezone token included).\n\nSchedule-only phrases:\n- Treat phrases like \"on the 15th\", \"last day of the month\", \"every Tuesday\", \"weekdays\", etc., as scheduling hints for the cron schedule. They do NOT make the timespan periodic by themselves.\n\n## 2. Identify Timezone  \n- If there is a timezone in the \"timespan\" field, use the corresponding 3 digit timezone code.\n- If no timezone is mentioned, use the \"timezone\" field. If this is not a 3-character timezone (eg. an offset like GMT+05:30), convert it to the appropriate 3 digit timezone code.\n- If the timezone field is empty and there is no timezone mentioned in the timespan field, use UTC.\n- Set executionTime to the resulting UTC time in zero-padded HH:MM format (no timezone token included).\n\nNote: Do not attempt to encode day-of-week, day-of-month, quarter, or year semantics in timespan; those are handled by the cron schedule.\n\n## 3. Identify Timespan Type (Periodic vs Static vs Unbounded)\n- Periodic timespan (emit GL_* codes) ONLY when the natural language describes a recurring reporting window tied to a fiscal boundary that is coarser than the schedule frequency. Examples:\n  - \"for the last 10 days before month end\", \"from D-5 to D+2\", \"during period close\", \"period end\", \"end of the quarter\", \"last two days of the quarter\".\n  - NOT periodic if the phrase merely specifies a calendar day like \"on the 15th\" or a time like \"at noon\" — those are scheduling details handled by the cron schedule.\n- Static timespan when a bounded finite interval is specified:\n  - \"next ten days\", \"the next 5 weeks\", \"2 months starting tomorrow\", explicit date ranges.\n- Unbounded timespan when no start or end date/window is specified:\n  - \"Execute this rule every day\", \"On the 15th at noon\", \"Every Tuesday at 9am\" (schedule-only instructions).\n\nFrequency alignment rule:\n- Only emit GL_PERIOD_END_DATE when the frequency is finer than monthly (ORA_HOURLY or ORA_DAILY) or the text explicitly asks for a period-relative window.\n- Only emit GL_QUARTER_END_DATE when the frequency is ORA_DAILY, ORA_WEEKLY, or ORA_MONTHLY (schedule is finer than the quarter boundary) or explicitly requested.\n- Only emit GL_YEAR_END_DATE when the frequency is ORA_DAILY, ORA_WEEKLY, ORA_MONTHLY, or ORA_QUARTERLY (schedule is finer than the year boundary) or explicitly requested.\n- If the provided frequency is equal to or coarser than the referenced boundary (e.g., ORA_MONTHLY with \"on the 15th\"), do NOT emit GL_*; return an unbounded timespan instead (\"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\").\n\n### Periodic Timespan Instructions & Examples\nConvert the input timespan into expressions that look like this: \"CODE +-x, CODE +-y\" ONLY when the text asks for a fiscal boundary window (period/quarter/year) and when that boundary is coarser than the provided frequency’s cadence. Otherwise, do not emit GL_*; prefer unbounded or static spans as appropriate.\nThe code should always be one of `GL_PERIOD_END_DATE`, `GL_QUARTER_END_DATE`, or `GL_YEAR_END_DATE`. You do NOT have access to any other codes.\nIf exactly one periodic bound is specified, then the other bound should be the appropriate \"D\" value. \"D\" refers to the fiscal period end date. When the frequency is ORA_QUARTERLY, or ORA_YEARLY, D is the fiscal quarter end date and fiscal year end date respectively.\nFollow the examples below for how to create the timespan object.\nYou must ALWAYS return a timespan object.\n\n- End of the month:\n\"timespan\": \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE\"\n  - This pattern applies for quarter end and year end as well.\n\n- Mid-month (emit periodic ONLY if the schedule frequency is finer than monthly or the text explicitly requires a period-relative window):\n  - If frequency = ORA_DAILY or ORA_HOURLY, and the request is period-relative (e.g., \"mid-month window\"):\n    \"timespan\": \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE -15, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE -15\"\n  - If frequency = ORA_MONTHLY and the request is \"on the 15th\" (calendar day), do NOT emit GL_*; return unbounded timespan.\n\n- Period End (GL_PERIOD_END_DATE):\n\"timespan\": \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE\"\n(The last day of the month)\n\n- Daily from day -5 to PE:\n\"timespan\": \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE -5, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE\"\n(Last 5 days leading up to period end)\n\n- 10 days to period close:\n\"timespan\": \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE -10, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE\"\n(Last 10 days of the month)\n\n- Quarter End:\n\"timespan\": \"GL_QUARTER_END_DATE, GL_QUARTER_END_DATE\"\n(The last day of the fiscal quarter)\n\nNote on timezone/day-boundary offsets:\n- If the requested boundary instant is specified at or near local midnight (e.g., “quarter end at midnight CET”), converting that local instant to UTC may shift the effective UTC date to the prior day. In such cases, adjust the periodic bounds by one day to represent the intended local boundary day:\n  - If local “midnight” falls on the UTC date before the quarter-end local date, use:\n    \"timespan\": \"GL_QUARTER_END_DATE -1, GL_QUARTER_END_DATE -1\"\n  - Similarly, apply ±1 adjustments to GL_PERIOD_END_DATE or GL_YEAR_END_DATE as needed.\n\n- For explicit periods like D-5 to D+5:\n\"timespan\": \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE -5, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE +5\"\n(D refers to period end here. So, all you have to do is substitute \"PE\" with \"D\". In the above example, it signifies that the timespan ranges from 5 days before period end to 5 days after period end.)\n\n- D+5:\n\"timespan\": \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE +5\"\n(Only one bound is specified so the other is inferred to be \"D\")\n\n- 5 days to year end\n\"timespan\": \"GL_YEAR_END_DATE -5, GL_YEAR_END_DATE\"\n(last 5 days of the year, other bound inferred to be the year end)\n\n## Static Timespan Instructions & Examples\nConvert the input timespan into a concrete start and end date as \"YYYY-MM-DD, YYYY-MM-DD\".\nFollow the instructions below for how to do this. Ensure that the start date is at or before the end date.\n\n1) Anchor date\n   - Explicit start date: use it.\n   - \"Starting today\": start = current_date.\n   - \"Starting tomorrow\": start = current_date + 1 day.\n   - \"Starting <weekday>\": next occurrence of that weekday on or after current_date (include today if it matches and \"starting\" implies inclusion).\n   - Otherwise: start = current_date.\n\n2) Inclusivity and unit expansion\n   - All static spans are inclusive at both ends.\n   - Days: end = start + (N - 1) days (respect \"starting tomorrow\" when present).\n   - Weeks: end = start + (7 × N) - 1 days.\n   - Months: end = the day before the same day-of-month after N months.\n     - Month-end handling: If the anchor day doesn't exist in the target month, roll to that month's last day, then subtract one day.\n\n3) Explicit ranges\n   - \"From X to Y\": start = X, end = Y, inclusive unless stated otherwise.\n\n### Unbounded Timespan Instructions & Examples\nReturn the timespan value \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\" when there is no explicit reporting window. This includes:\n- Pure schedule requests (e.g., \"On the 15th at noon\", \"Every Tuesday at 9am\").\n- Any case where the natural language does not request a fiscal boundary window or a fixed date range.\n\n## 4. Output JSON Format:\n\nYou MUST make sure to include a timespan.\nIt is NEVER correct to have timespan be an empty string.\nNEVER REPEAT THE JSON, that'd break the parsing logic. There should only be a single JSON object in the output. \n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"<identified execution time in HH:MM format>\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"<identified timespan. THIS MUST NOT BE EMPTY>\",\n    \"periodic\": \"<true if periodic expression, false otherwise>\"\n}\n\n## Examples:\nFor all examples, the current date is 2025-09-22 and cron executes in UTC. The executionTime below is always the final UTC time after converting from the interpreted local time.\n\n### Example 1: periodic timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\" : \"end of the month\",\n    \"frequency\" : \"ORA_MONTHLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\" : \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\" : \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\" : \"GL_PERIOD_END_DATE, GL_PERIOD_END_DATE\",\n    \"periodic\" : true\n}\n```\n\n### Example 2: periodic timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"last two days of the quarter\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_QUARTERLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"GL_QUARTER_END_DATE -1, GL_QUARTER_END_DATE\",\n    \"periodic\": true\n}\n```\n\n### Example 3: static timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"Next ten days\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_HOURLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"2025-09-22, 2025-10-01\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\n\n### Example 4a: schedule-only (unbounded) with monthly frequency\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"On the 15th at noon\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_MONTHLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"12:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\nExplanation: \"On the 15th\" is handled by the cron schedule in the day-of-month field. No fiscal boundary window is requested; timespan is unbounded.\n\n### Example 4b: static timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"next 5 weeks starting tomorrow\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_MONTHLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"2025-09-23, 2025-10-27\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\n\n### Example 5: unbounded timespan (minute-of-hour)\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"Every hour at the 20th minute\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_HOURLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:20\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\n\n### Example 6: unbounded timespan with explicit timezone (converted to UTC)\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"11:00 PST\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_DAILY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"19:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\nExplanation: 11:00 PST converts to 19:00 UTC (standard offset UTC−08:00).\n\n### Example 7: periodic timespan with offset due to timezone conversion\nInput:\n```json\n{\n  \"timespan\": \"Quarter end at midnight CET\",\n  \"frequency\": \"ORA_QUARTERLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n  \"executionTime\": \"23:00\",\n  \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n  \"timespan\": \"GL_QUARTER_END_DATE -1, GL_QUARTER_END_DATE -1\",\n  \"periodic\": true\n}\n```\nExplanation: Midnight CET at the start of the quarter-end day corresponds to 23:00 UTC on the previous calendar day, so both bounds are offset by -1 to align with the intended local quarter-end date.\n\n## Guardrails:\n- Never emit GL_* codes solely because the input specifies a calendar day or weekday; that belongs to derive_cron.\n- Emit GL_* only for true fiscal boundary windows (period/quarter/year) and only when that boundary is coarser than the provided frequency, unless the text explicitly demands a fiscal-relative window.\n  - Do NOT use any codes other than the provided `GL_PERIOD_END_DATE`, `GL_QUARTER_END_DATE`, or `GL_YEAR_END_DATE`.\n- If in doubt and no explicit reporting window is stated, choose unbounded: \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\"."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Scheduled Time Derivation"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1768284503560"
          },
          "code": "SCHEDULED_TIME_DERIVATION",
          "id": "1772839528924",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1772839528924_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are given:\n- Natural language user input: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timespan}}\n- Frequency code: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.frequency}} (one of: ORA_HOURLY, ORA_DAILY, ORA_WEEKLY, ORA_MONTHLY, ORA_QUARTERLY, ORA_YEARLY) (if the frequency code is not provided or is not an exact match, you should infer the appropriate frequency code)\n- Default time zone: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}} (user’s browser timezone; interpret times in this timezone unless the user explicitly specifies a different timezone, in which case use the specified timezone. Do not include any timezone in the output.)\n- Context objects for fiscal calendar:\n  - Current date and time: {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}}\n  - Current period details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_CURRENT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output}}\n  - Next period details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_NEXT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output}}\n  - First period of next quarter details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_FIRST_PERIOD_NEXT_QUARTER_DETAILS.$output}}\n  - First period of next year details: {{$context.$nodes.FIN_FUN_GET_FIRST_PERIOD_NEXT_YEAR_DETAILS.$output}}\n\nGoal:\nReturn a single scheduledTime string with exactly 7 comma-separated fields, no extra spaces:\ntimeOfDay,dayOfWeek,dayOfMonth,weekOfMonth,monthOfYear,startingMonthOfQuarter,intervalValue\n\nOracle Fusion GL semantics for “D+-m, D+-n” style inputs:\n- Interpret “D” as the fiscal boundary date relevant to the frequency:\n  - ORA_HOURLY / ORA_DAILY / ORA_WEEKLY / ORA_MONTHLY: D = end of the fiscal period.\n  - ORA_QUARTERLY: D = end of the fiscal quarter.\n  - ORA_YEARLY: D = end of the fiscal year.\n- Use the provided context objects to resolve the boundary date:\n  - Fiscal period end: use endDate from Current period details by default; if the input clearly references the next period, use Next period details.endDate.\n  - Fiscal quarter: quarterStartDate indicates quarter start; the quarter end is the endDate of the third month in that quarter. If “next quarter” is implied, use First period of next quarter details for its start.\n  - Fiscal year: yearStartDate indicates fiscal year start; the year end is the last period’s endDate within that fiscal year. If only the first-period-of-next-year is available, infer the current fiscal year end as the month prior to that start.\n\nOutput construction rules (always ensure a valid 7-field string):\n1) timeOfDay:\n- Interpret user intent in a local timezone, then convert that intended local time to UTC because the cron executes in UTC.\n- Timezone selection:\n  - If the user explicitly specifies a timezone, use it.\n  - Otherwise use {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}}.\n  - If neither is available or parsing fails, default to UTC.\n- Parse a 24h HH:mm time (local) from the user input. Recognize numeric and natural-language forms, including:\n  - “8am”, “20:15”, “5:30 pm”, “noon” (12:00), “midnight” (00:00)\n  - Minute-of-hour phrases (assume hour=00 when only a minute is given):\n    - “the 5th minute of every hour” => 00:05\n    - “a quarter past the hour” => 00:15\n    - “half past the hour” => 00:30\n    - “a quarter to the hour” => 00:45\n    - “x minutes past the hour” => 00:MM\n    - “x minutes to the hour” => 00:(60−x), zero-padded\n    - “top of the hour” => 00:00\n- If no time is found, default local time to 00:00.\n- Convert the interpreted local time to UTC, then output the resulting UTC HH:mm (zero-padded, no timezone).\n- Date-boundary adjustments when converting to UTC:\n  - ORA_WEEKLY: if the local-to-UTC conversion shifts the day (e.g., local Monday 00:30 becomes Sunday 23:30 UTC), adjust dayOfWeek accordingly to reflect the UTC day.\n  - ORA_MONTHLY / ORA_QUARTERLY / ORA_YEARLY: derive the target local calendar date first (e.g., the “D” boundary day for the relevant period/quarter/year). Combine that local date with the local time, convert to UTC, and if the conversion crosses to the previous/next UTC day, adjust dayOfMonth (and monthOfYear for YEARLY) to the resulting UTC date so that the cron fires at the intended local wall-clock time.\n  - ORA_HOURLY: only the minute is used by the cron; use the UTC minute from the conversion (minute usually remains the same).\n- Output format must be exactly HH:mm with zero-padding and no timezone.\n\n2) dayOfWeek:\n- Accepted tokens: SUN, MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT. The validator also accepts comma-separated lists and ranges like MON,WED or MON-FRI.\n- Interpret user input generously for non-technical phrasing (e.g., “weekdays”, “weekends”, “business days”, “every Tue & Thu”, “Mon-Fri”, “Tue,Thu”):\n  - Map phrases to the equivalent set (e.g., “weekdays”/“business days” => MON-FRI; “weekends” => SAT,SUN).\n  - From any list/range/set mentioned, select a single representative day deterministically: choose the first day in week order MON,TUE,WED,THU,FRI,SAT,SUN that belongs to the interpreted set.\n- For ORA_WEEKLY: output exactly one single day token (required by the function). If nothing is found, default to MON.\n- For all other frequencies: output “-”.\n\n3) dayOfMonth:\n- For ORA_MONTHLY, ORA_QUARTERLY, ORA_YEARLY:\n  - If the user specifies an explicit day (e.g., “on the 15th”, “day 3”), use 1–31.\n  - If the user clearly requests the last day of the month (e.g., “last day of the month”, “end of month”), set dayOfMonth to “L”.\n  - Otherwise, apply “D” semantics:\n    - Monthly: use the day number from the resolved fiscal period end date. If the day number from the fiscal period end date is greater than 28, set it to L.\n    - Quarterly: use the day number of the quarter-end date. If the day number from the fiscal quarter end date is greater than 28, set it to L.\n    - Yearly: use the day number of the fiscal year-end date.\n  - If still ambiguous, default to L.\n- For ORA_HOURLY, ORA_DAILY, ORA_WEEKLY: output “-”.\n\n4) weekOfMonth:\n- Not required; output “-” unless the user clearly specifies first/second/third/fourth/fifth (1–5). If unclear, output “-”.\n\n5) monthOfYear:\n- For ORA_YEARLY:\n  - If the user specifies a month, convert it to JAN..DEC and use it.\n  - Otherwise, set to the month (JAN..DEC) of the resolved fiscal year-end date.\n  - If ambiguous, default to JAN.\n- For other frequencies: output “-”.\n\n6) startingMonthOfQuarter:\n- For ORA_QUARTERLY:\n  - If the user specifies a starting month, convert to JAN..DEC and use it.\n  - Otherwise, derive from the last month of the current quarter (current quarter by default; next quarter if clearly indicated).\n  - If unavailable or ambiguous, default to JAN.\n- For other frequencies: output “-”.\n\n7) intervalValue:\n- For all listed ORA_* frequencies, output “-”.\n\nGeneral parsing guidance:\n- Normalize day tokens to uppercase (SUN..SAT). Normalize month names or numbers to JAN..DEC.\n- Ignore any timezone token in the final output (timezone affects interpretation only).\n- If the user is ambiguous, contradictory, or invalid, use the defaults above to ensure a syntactically valid 7-field result.\n\nFrequency-specific minimums:\n- ORA_HOURLY: requires timeOfDay; others “-”.\n- ORA_DAILY: requires timeOfDay; others “-”.\n- ORA_WEEKLY: requires timeOfDay and exactly one single dayOfWeek token.\n- ORA_MONTHLY: requires timeOfDay and dayOfMonth.\n- ORA_QUARTERLY: requires timeOfDay, dayOfMonth, and startingMonthOfQuarter.\n- ORA_YEARLY: requires timeOfDay, dayOfMonth, and monthOfYear.\n\nOutput format:\n- Return a single JSON object with exactly one key named scheduleTime.\n- The value must be the 7-field scheduledTime string with no extra spaces inside the string and no surrounding commentary.\n\nExact shape:\n{\n  \"scheduleTime\": \"HH:mm,DAY_OR_-,DOM_OR_-,WEEK_OR_-,MON_OR_-,STARTQ_OR_-,INTERVAL_OR_-\"\n}\n\nConstraints:\n- No additional keys, metadata, or wrapping text.\n- The scheduledTime string must have exactly 7 comma-separated fields and follow all rules above.\n- dayOfMonth may be either 1–31 or “L” (for last day of the month) when the frequency requires dayOfMonth (MONTHLY, QUARTERLY, YEARLY)."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Payments Timespan AND Frequency Extraction"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1774889798418"
          },
          "code": "PAYMENTS_TIMESPAN_AND_FREQUENCY_EXTRACTION",
          "id": "1774889755722",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1774889755724_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "# Timespan & Frequency Extraction\n\n## Input\n\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\" : \"{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timespan}}\",\n    \"frequency\" : \"{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.frequency}}\",\n    \"timezone\" : \"{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}}\"\n}\n```\n\n## System Context:\nCurrent system date and time:  {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}}\n\nParse natural-language for:\n- Schedule execution time\n- Timezone\n- Derived timespan (this MUST be included)\nYou will support deriving periodic timespans (representing recurring or relative schedules), static timespans (with a fixed start and end date), and unbounded timespans (not periodic and no start or end date).\n\nMake sure to think through your response. Ensure that it has all of the necessary components--none of them should be empty strings.\n\n## Steps\n\n## 1. Identify Execution Time\n- Resolve the source timezone for interpretation:\n  - If the user explicitly specifies a timezone in the \"timespan\" text (e.g., PST, CET, UTC±hh:mm, IANA like America/Los_Angeles), use that.\n  - Otherwise, use {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}}.\n  - If neither is available or parsing fails, default to UTC.\n- Parse a local wall-clock time from the \"timespan\" text, recognizing numeric and natural-language forms:\n  - Numeric: “8am”, “08:00”, “20:15”, “5:30 pm”, “noon” => 12:00, “midnight” => 00:00.\n  - Minute-of-hour phrases (assume hour=00 when only a minute is given):\n    - “the 5th minute of every hour” => 00:05\n    - “a quarter past the hour” => 00:15\n    - “half past the hour” => 00:30\n    - “a quarter to the hour” => 00:45\n    - “x minutes past the hour” => 00:MM\n    - “x minutes to the hour” => 00:(60−x), zero-padded\n    - “top of the hour” => 00:00\n- If no time is found, default the local time to 00:00.\n- Convert the interpreted local time to UTC (cron executes in UTC):\n  - Apply the resolved timezone (including any DST rules) to the parsed local time.\n  - Convert the result to UTC.\n- Set executionTime to the resulting UTC time in zero-padded HH:MM format (no timezone token included).\n\n## 2. Identify Timezone  \n- If there is a timezone in the \"timespan\" field, use the corresponding 3 digit timezone code.\n- If no timezone is mentioned, use the \"timezone\" field. If this is not a 3-character timezone (eg. an offset like GMT+05:30), convert it to the appropriate 3 digit timezone code.\n- If the timezone field is empty and there is no timezone mentioned in the timespan field, use UTC.\n\n## 3. Identify if Timespan is Static, Dynamic, or Unbounded\n- Classify the timespan as dynamic if it has phrases describing reporting windows or recurring time periods:  \n  - \"for the last 10 days before month end\"\n  - \"during period close\"\n  - \"mid-month\", \"last Tuesday of month\", \"from D-5 to D+2\"\n- Classify the timespan as static if it is asking for a bounded or finite time interval:\n  - \"next ten days\"\n  - \"the next 5 weeks\"\n  - \"2 months starting tomorrow\"\n- Classify the timepsan as unbounded if it does not specify a start time and does not specify an end time:\n  - \"Execute this rule every day\"\n  - \"Execute this rule at the 30th minute of every hour\"\n\n### Periodic Timespan Instructions & Examples  \nConvert the input timespan into expressions that look like this: \"((CODE) +-x, (CODE) +-y)\".\nThe code should always be one of `GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE`, `GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE`, or `GREGORIAN_YEAR_END_DATE`. You do NOT have access to any other codes.\nIf exactly one periodic bound is specified, then the other bound should be the appropriate \"D\" value.\nFollow the examples below for how to create the timespan object.\nYou must ALWAYS return a timespan object.\n\n- End of the month:\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE\"\n  - This pattern applies for quarter end and year end as well.\n\n- Mid-month:\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE -15, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE -15\"\n(A single day: 15 days before the end of the month)\n\n- Period End (GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE):\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE\"\n(The last day of the month)\n\n- Daily from day -5 to PE:\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE -5, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE\"\n(Last 5 days leading up to period end)\n\n- 10 days to period close:\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE -10, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE\"\n(Last 10 days of the month)\n\n- Quarter End:\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE, GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE\"\n(The last day of the current quarter: Mar 31, Jun 30, Sep 30, Dec 31)\n\n- For explicit periods like D-5 to D+5:\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE -5, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE +5\"\n(D refers to period end here. So, all you have to do is substitute \"PE\" with \"D\". In the above example, it signifies that the timespan ranges from 5 days before period end to 5 days after period end.)\n\n- D+5:\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE +5\"\n(Only one bound is specified so the other is inferred to be \"D\")\n\n- D-1 (Frequency = Quarterly)\n\"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE -1, GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE\"\n(Only one bound is specified so the other is inferred to be \"D\")\n\n- NOTE: \"D\" has a different meaning based on frequency.  For instance, \nContext = LedgerAgent, Frequency = Hourly/Daily/Weekly/Monthly, D means GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE\nContext = LedgerAgent, Frequency = Quarterly, D means GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE\nContext = LedgerAgent, Frequency = Yearly, D means GREGORIAN_YEAR_END_DATE\n\n### Static Timespan Instructions & Examples\nConvert the input timespan into a concrete start and end date as \"YYYY-MM-DD, YYYY-MM-DD\".\nFollow the instructions below for how to do this. Ensure that the start date is at or before the end date.\n\n1) Anchor date\n   - Explicit start date: use it.\n   - \"Starting today\": start = current_date.\n   - \"Starting tomorrow\": start = current_date + 1 day.\n   - \"Starting <weekday>\": next occurrence of that weekday on or after current_date (include today if it matches and \"starting\" implies inclusion).\n   - Otherwise: start = current_date.\n\n2) Inclusivity and unit expansion\n   - All static spans are inclusive at both ends.\n   - Days: end = start + (N - 1) days (respect \"starting tomorrow\" when present).\n   - Weeks: end = start + (7 × N) - 1 days.\n   - Months: end = the day before the same day-of-month after N months.\n     - Month-end handling: If the anchor day doesn't exist in the target month, roll to that month's last day, then subtract one day.\n\n3) Explicit ranges\n   - \"From X to Y\": start = X, end = Y, inclusive unless stated otherwise.\n\n### Unbounded Timespan Instructions & Examples\nReturn the timespan value \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\".\nThis only applies if there is no mention of a start or end date.\n\n## 4. Output JSON Format:\n\nYou MUST make sure to include a timespan.\nIt is NEVER correct to have timespan be an empty string.\nNEVER REPEAT THE JSON, that'd break the parsing logic. There should only be a single JSON object in the output. \n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"<identified execution time in HH:MM format>\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"<identified timespan. THIS MUST NOT BE EMPTY>\",\n    \"periodic\": \"<true if periodic expression, false otherwise>\"\n}\n```\n\n## Examples:\nFor all examples, the current date is 2025-09-22 and cron executes in UTC. The executionTime below is always the final UTC time after converting from the interpreted local time.\n\n### Example 1: periodic timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\" : \"end of the month\",\n    \"frequency\" : \"ORA_MONTHLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\" : \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\" : \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\" : \"GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE, GREGORIAN_MONTH_END_DATE\",\n    \"periodic\" : true\n}\n```\n\n### Example 2: periodic timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"last two days of the quarter\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_QUARTERLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE -1, GREGORIAN_QUARTER_END_DATE\",\n    \"periodic\": true\n}\n```\n\n### Example 3: static timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"Next ten days\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_HOURLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"2025-09-22, 2025-10-01\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\n\n### Example 4: static timespan\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"next 5 weeks starting tomorrow\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_MONTHLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"2025-09-23, 2025-10-27\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\n\n### Example 5: unbounded timespan (minute-of-hour)\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"Every hour at the 20th minute\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_HOURLY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"00:20\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\n\n### Example 6: unbounded timespan with explicit timezone (converted to UTC)\nInput:\n```json\n{\n    \"timespan\": \"11:00 PST\",\n    \"frequency\": \"ORA_DAILY\"\n}\n```\nOutput:\n```json\n{\n    \"executionTime\": \"19:00\",\n    \"timezone\": \"UTC\",\n    \"timespan\": \"{{$context.$system.$currentDate}}, null\",\n    \"periodic\": false\n}\n```\nExplanation: 11:00 PST converts to 19:00 UTC (standard offset UTC−08:00)."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
