            {
              "id": "1774889798420_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are given:\n- Natural language user input: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timespan}}\n- Frequency code: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.frequency}} (one of: ORA_HOURLY, ORA_DAILY, ORA_WEEKLY, ORA_MONTHLY, ORA_QUARTERLY, ORA_YEARLY) (if the frequency code is not provided or is not an exact match, you should infer the appropriate frequency code)\n- Default time zone: {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}} (user’s browser timezone; interpret times in this timezone unless the user explicitly specifies a different timezone, in which case use the specified timezone. Do not include any timezone in the output.)\n- Context objects for fiscal calendar:\n  - Current date and time: {{$context.$system.$currentDateTime}}\n  - Current period details: {{$context.$nodes.ORA_FIN_FUN_GET_CURRENT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output}}\n  - Next period details: {{$context.$nodes.ORA_FIN_FUN_GET_NEXT_PERIOD_DETAILS.$output}}\n  - First period of next quarter details: {{$context.$nodes.ORA_FIN_FUN_GET_FIRST_PERIOD_NEXT_QUARTER_DETAILS.$output}}\n  - First period of next year details: {{$context.$nodes.ORA_FIN_FUN_GET_FIRST_PERIOD_NEXT_YEAR_DETAILS.$output}}\n\nGoal:\nReturn a single scheduledTime string with exactly 7 comma-separated fields, no extra spaces:\ntimeOfDay,dayOfWeek,dayOfMonth,weekOfMonth,monthOfYear,startingMonthOfQuarter,intervalValue\n\nOracle Fusion GL semantics for “D+-m, D+-n” style inputs:\n- Interpret “D” as the fiscal boundary date relevant to the frequency:\n  - ORA_HOURLY / ORA_DAILY / ORA_WEEKLY / ORA_MONTHLY: D = end of the fiscal period.\n  - ORA_QUARTERLY: D = end of the fiscal quarter.\n  - ORA_YEARLY: D = end of the fiscal year.\n- Use the provided context objects to resolve the boundary date:\n  - Fiscal period end: use endDate from Current period details by default; if the input clearly references the next period, use Next period details.endDate.\n  - Fiscal quarter: quarterStartDate indicates quarter start; the quarter end is the endDate of the third month in that quarter. If “next quarter” is implied, use First period of next quarter details for its start.\n  - Fiscal year: yearStartDate indicates fiscal year start; the year end is the last period’s endDate within that fiscal year. If only the first-period-of-next-year is available, infer the current fiscal year end as the month prior to that start.\n\nOutput construction rules (always ensure a valid 7-field string):\n1) timeOfDay:\n- Interpret user intent in a local timezone, then convert that intended local time to UTC because the cron executes in UTC.\n- Timezone selection:\n  - If the user explicitly specifies a timezone, use it.\n  - Otherwise use {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.timezone}}.\n  - If neither is available or parsing fails, default to UTC.\n- Parse a 24h HH:mm time (local) from the user input. Recognize numeric and natural-language forms, including:\n  - “8am”, “20:15”, “5:30 pm”, “noon” (12:00), “midnight” (00:00)\n  - Minute-of-hour phrases (assume hour=00 when only a minute is given):\n    - “the 5th minute of every hour” => 00:05\n    - “a quarter past the hour” => 00:15\n    - “half past the hour” => 00:30\n    - “a quarter to the hour” => 00:45\n    - “x minutes past the hour” => 00:MM\n    - “x minutes to the hour” => 00:(60−x), zero-padded\n    - “top of the hour” => 00:00\n- If no time is found, default local time to 00:00.\n- Convert the interpreted local time to UTC, then output the resulting UTC HH:mm (zero-padded, no timezone).\n- Date-boundary adjustments when converting to UTC:\n  - ORA_WEEKLY: if the local-to-UTC conversion shifts the day (e.g., local Monday 00:30 becomes Sunday 23:30 UTC), adjust dayOfWeek accordingly to reflect the UTC day.\n  - ORA_MONTHLY / ORA_QUARTERLY / ORA_YEARLY: derive the target local calendar date first (e.g., the “D” boundary day for the relevant period/quarter/year). Combine that local date with the local time, convert to UTC, and if the conversion crosses to the previous/next UTC day, adjust dayOfMonth (and monthOfYear for YEARLY) to the resulting UTC date so that the cron fires at the intended local wall-clock time.\n  - ORA_HOURLY: only the minute is used by the cron; use the UTC minute from the conversion (minute usually remains the same).\n- Output format must be exactly HH:mm with zero-padding and no timezone.\n\n2) dayOfWeek:\n- Accepted tokens: SUN, MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT. The validator also accepts comma-separated lists and ranges like MON,WED or MON-FRI.\n- Interpret user input generously for non-technical phrasing (e.g., “weekdays”, “weekends”, “business days”, “every Tue & Thu”, “Mon-Fri”, “Tue,Thu”):\n  - Map phrases to the equivalent set (e.g., “weekdays”/“business days” => MON-FRI; “weekends” => SAT,SUN).\n  - From any list/range/set mentioned, select a single representative day deterministically: choose the first day in week order MON,TUE,WED,THU,FRI,SAT,SUN that belongs to the interpreted set.\n- For ORA_WEEKLY: output exactly one single day token (required by the function). If nothing is found, default to MON.\n- For all other frequencies: output “-”.\n\n3) dayOfMonth:\n- For ORA_MONTHLY, ORA_QUARTERLY, ORA_YEARLY:\n  - If the user specifies an explicit day (e.g., “on the 15th”, “day 3”), use 1–31.\n  - Otherwise, apply “D” semantics:\n    - Monthly: use the day number from the resolved fiscal period endDate.\n    - Quarterly: use the day number of the quarter-end date.\n    - Yearly: use the day number of the fiscal year-end date.\n  - If still ambiguous, default to 1.\n- For ORA_HOURLY, ORA_DAILY, ORA_WEEKLY: output “-”.\n\n4) weekOfMonth:\n- Not required; output “-” unless the user clearly specifies first/second/third/fourth/fifth (1–5). If unclear, output “-”.\n\n5) monthOfYear:\n- For ORA_YEARLY:\n  - If the user specifies a month, convert it to JAN..DEC and use it.\n  - Otherwise, set to the month (JAN..DEC) of the resolved fiscal year-end date.\n  - If ambiguous, default to JAN.\n- For other frequencies: output “-”.\n\n6) startingMonthOfQuarter:\n- For ORA_QUARTERLY:\n  - If the user specifies a starting month, convert to JAN..DEC and use it.\n  - Otherwise, derive from the relevant quarter’s quarterStartDate (current quarter by default; next quarter if clearly indicated).\n  - If unavailable or ambiguous, default to JAN.\n- For other frequencies: output “-”.\n\n7) intervalValue:\n- For all listed ORA_* frequencies, output “-”.\n\nGeneral parsing guidance:\n- Normalize day tokens to uppercase (SUN..SAT). Normalize month names or numbers to JAN..DEC.\n- Ignore any timezone token in the final output (timezone affects interpretation only).\n- If the user is ambiguous, contradictory, or invalid, use the defaults above to ensure a syntactically valid 7-field result.\n\nFrequency-specific minimums:\n- ORA_HOURLY: requires timeOfDay; others “-”.\n- ORA_DAILY: requires timeOfDay; others “-”.\n- ORA_WEEKLY: requires timeOfDay and exactly one single dayOfWeek token.\n- ORA_MONTHLY: requires timeOfDay and dayOfMonth.\n- ORA_QUARTERLY: requires timeOfDay, dayOfMonth, and startingMonthOfQuarter.\n- ORA_YEARLY: requires timeOfDay, dayOfMonth, and monthOfYear.\n\nOutput format:\n- Return a single JSON object with exactly one key named scheduleTime.\n- The value must be the 7-field scheduledTime string with no extra spaces inside the string and no surrounding commentary.\n\nExact shape:\n{\n  \"scheduleTime\": \"HH:mm,DAY_OR_-,DOM_OR_-,WEEK_OR_-,MON_OR_-,STARTQ_OR_-,INTERVAL_OR_-\"\n}\n\nConstraints:\n- No additional keys, metadata, or wrapping text.\n- The scheduledTime string must have exactly 7 comma-separated fields and follow all rules above."
